  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe MCS bringing us this past evenings activity has passed over \nthe TN/GA border with light stratiform rain persisting across\nportions of the forecast area in its wake. Overall, the threat \nfor organized strong to severe storms and new flash flooding has \ndiminished for the overnight, however, be cautious for any \nlingering flood issues as we continue into these early morning \nhours.\n\nAdditional periods of showers and storms are expected today through \nFriday as we remain in a predominant northwest flow pattern beneath \nthe transition zone of a strong ridge axis to our west and a trough \naxis to our north and east. Comparing latest radar trends to the 00Z \nHREF Suite, NAM3km seems to be initializing the best with some \nadditional CI and an associated vort lobe across eastern Indiana. \n\nThe CAMs that do seem to depict this activity to a varying extent \nsuggest some additional scattered showers and thunderstorms move \nthrough the area by about 12Z this morning. Quite honestly, I have \nlittle confidence in how this convection may play out. That being \nsaid, we have some very low 1hr FFGs between 0.9 to 1.3 inches due\nto the evolution of this past evenings precip. Morning \ninstability should be fairly limited, so the strong to severe \nthreat is low but perhaps not zero depending how the intensity of \nthe disturbance evolves. The main threat may be focused on the \npotential for some additional flash flooding, particularly for \nthose very worked over locations in our central and northern \nareas. Have introduced a short fuse Flash Flood Watch due to the \npoor antecedent conditions. \n\nCAMs then generally want to dry us out for much of the mid-afternoon,\nwith an additional round of showers and storms possible in the \nlate evening to early overnight. Cannot rule out some additional \nflooding concerns or even the potential for isolated strong to \nsevere storms depending on how well we destabilize in the \nafternoon. Did not feel confident enough in how this second round \nof activity could develop to cover that with the Flash Flood \nWatch, but depending on how things evolve it isn't out of the \nrealm of possibility an extension is needed. \n\nAgain, additional periods of showers and storms are possible \nThursday and Friday. The shortwaves look to be relatively weaker so \nthe potential for strong/svr storms seems relatively lower, though \nnot zero. We will also start to see an increase in upper-level \nheights by Friday and this will translate to a slight warming \ntrend. Central and southern areas may be looking at Heat Advisory \ncriteria as we head into this late week/weekend period. While we \ncannot totally rule out some diurnal pops, additional organized \nconvection is unlikely. This pattern is expected to continue into \nnext week extent but perhaps with temperatures a few degrees\ncooler comparative to the weekend.",
